 "issues": [
  {
   "id": "V1",
   "path": "sections.analysis.1",
   "category": "facts",
   "severity": "fix",
   "problem": "The English text calls the horse-and-mule allowance \"this clause\" (the Chinese has 这一条). That implies a written provision. The same paragraph, and the evidence, say it was not in the written terms: Grant said he would instruct the officers left behind. The wording contradicts the paragraph's own caution and the article's central point.",
   "evidence": [
    {
     "id": "GRANT671-E17",
     "quote": "I told him that as the terms were written they would not"
    },
    {
     "id": "GRANT671-E18",
     "quote": "I would, therefore, instruct the officers I left behind to receive the paroles of his troops to let every man of the Confederate army who claimed to own a horse or mule take the animal to his home"
    }
   ],
   "proposed": "Change en \"this clause addressed\" to \"this item addressed\", and zh \"这一条针对的\" to \"这一项针对的\"."
  },
  {
   "id": "V2",
   "path": "sections.story.1",
   "category": "attribution",
   "severity": "fix",
   "problem": "In the English, \"Lee was a man of much dignity with an impassible face\" is stated in the narrator's voice. It is Grant's description. The Chinese keeps the attribution (按他的描述), so the two versions also differ.",
   "evidence": [
    {
     "id": "GRANT671-E10",
     "quote": "As he was a man of much dignity, with an impassible face, it was impossible to say"
    }
   ],
   "proposed": "Change en to \"In Grant's description, Lee was a man of much dignity with an impassible face; it was impossible to say…\"."
  }
 ],
 "overall": "The final draft is generally well supported by the evidence. Its quotations are verbatim, its interpretations are labeled, and the modern scenarios do not pose as records. Only two small fixes are needed: the wording that calls the horse-and-mule allowance a clause, and a missing attribution in the English of story.1."
}